WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.862
Can a security system catch an attack that
nobody has ever labelled?

2
00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:08.348
Not a variant of something it's seen. Not
a near miss. Something genuinely new.

3
00:00:08.348 --> 00:00:12.650
That one question is my entire
dissertation. And I'll be honest with you:
I

4
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:14.026
did not like the answer.

5
00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:15.705
Here's why it matters.

6
00:00:15.705 --> 00:00:20.162
Every second, thousands of connections hit
a network. Almost all of them are

7
00:00:20.162 --> 00:00:22.390
ordinary. A few of them are an attack.

8
00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.762
The way we normally find them is to show a
model millions of labelled attacks,

9
00:00:26.762 --> 00:00:28.449
and let it learn the difference.

10
00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:31.270
And it works. Ninety eight percent of
attacks, caught.

11
00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.023
But every single one of those was labelled
by a human first. Someone had already

12
00:00:36.023 --> 00:00:38.138
seen that attack, and written it down.

13
00:00:38.398 --> 00:00:42.946
So what happens on the day something
arrives that nobody has written down yet?

14
00:00:43.406 --> 00:00:47.034
That's the gap my dissertation goes after.
And here's the idea.

15
00:00:47.034 --> 00:00:51.519
What if you pair that trained model with a
second one that has only ever seen

16
00:00:51.519 --> 00:00:55.946
normal traffic? It has no idea what an
attack looks like. It only knows what

17
00:00:55.946 --> 00:00:56.995
normal looks like.

18
00:00:56.995 --> 00:01:01.524
It's called an autoencoder. Train it on
nothing but ordinary connections, then

19
00:01:01.524 --> 00:01:03.789
ask it to rebuild each one from memory.

20
00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:08.338
Normal traffic? It rebuilds it almost
perfectly. Something strange? It fumbles.

21
00:01:08.338 --> 00:01:11.101
And that fumble is your alarm. No labels
needed.

22
00:01:11.501 --> 00:01:14.917
So does it actually work? I tested it as
harshly as I could.

23
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:19.330
I deleted entire families of attack from
the training data. Worms. Backdoors.

24
00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:23.456
Fuzzers. The model had never seen a single
one. Then I threw them at it.

25
00:01:23.796 --> 00:01:25.035
And the answer was no.

26
00:01:25.035 --> 00:01:29.522
The hybrid scored zero point nine eight
three six. The plain model, on its own,

27
00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.850
scored zero point nine eight three seven.

28
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.398
Identical. Eight experiments. Not one of
them moved the needle.

29
00:01:35.398 --> 00:01:38.327
Which is not what I wanted to find, three
months in.

30
00:01:38.747 --> 00:01:40.644
But then I found where it does win.

31
00:01:40.644 --> 00:01:44.966
Starve it of labels. Give the trained
model fifty examples instead of a hundred

32
00:01:44.966 --> 00:01:46.389
and seventy five thousand.

33
00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:50.701
Suddenly the hybrid is two full points of
AUC ahead. And with no labels at all,

34
00:01:50.701 --> 00:01:54.031
the anomaly detector on its own still
reaches ninety percent.

35
00:01:54.031 --> 00:01:58.042
By two hundred and fifty examples, that
advantage has completely vanished.

36
00:01:58.402 --> 00:02:02.455
So the real answer isn't that hybrids are
better. It's sharper than that.

37
00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:06.714
Hybrids matter on day one. In the weeks
before anyone has labelled anything.

38
00:02:06.714 --> 00:02:08.563
After that, just use your labels.

39
00:02:08.563 --> 00:02:12.823
That's not the answer I went looking for.
But it's the one the data gave me,

40
00:02:12.823 --> 00:02:14.504
and it's the one I can defend.
